1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Adding a required field without inventing history. Rehearse the source_channel migration, then decide

2
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:11,500
The new requirement: record whether each request came by web, phone, or mobile.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,900
Guardrails: lock_timeout limits waiting for a lock; statement_timeout limits run time.

4
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:18,400
The column appears, NULL on all 12 rows: the old tickets never recorded a channel.

5
00:00:18,900 --> 00:00:22,800
ADD COLUMN takes a strong lock. Even a plain read in another session waits until we finish.

6
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:27,500
Backfill 'unknown', not 'web'. Ticket 1001's note mentions a mobile form, but one clue can't label every row.

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:30,700
The named CHECK validates all 12 existing rows: 'unknown' is allowed.

8
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:35,100
CHECK limits the words. NOT NULL requires a value. DEFAULT fills a value a writer leaves out.

9
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:39,300
Verify before deciding: inside the transaction, unknown = 12.

10
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:42,300
ROLLBACK removes the column, the rules, and the backfill together, and releases the lock.

11
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:46,200
To keep it, run the same verified block with COMMIT. Run it once.

12
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:49,600
Suppose the migration is committed. Now test the writers, each in a transaction you roll back.

13
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:52,800
An old INSERT that omits the column still works: DEFAULT supplies 'unknown'.

14
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:56,000
Omitting a column and sending NULL are different. NOT NULL refuses the NULL.

15
00:00:56,500 --> 00:00:59,200
'mobile' is allowed. We roll it back rather than invent 1004's history.

16
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:01,500
'fax' is refused by the named CHECK. The stored value is unchanged.

17
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,000
The database can enforce an allowed word. It can't prove the word is true.

18
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:09,000
During the rehearsal, rollback is a clean undo.

19
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:12,000
After commit, an 'opposite command' is not automatically a recovery plan.

20
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:15,000
On a large, busy table, plan batches, lock time, and deployment order too. Check current docs.

21
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:26,600
Remember: Historical rows get 'unknown': don't invent facts nobody recorded. CHECK limits words, NOT NULL requires a value, DEFAULT fills omissions. Verify structure AND meaning before choosing COMMIT or ROLLBACK. Transactional DDL is reversible, but its locks still make others wait. After real data arrives, prefer a forward fix to dropping the column.

